sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant A as API command A
    participant B as API command B
    participant D as PostgreSQL
    Note over A,D: Row selector: customer_billing_state.customer_id = C<br/>Request advisory identity: api_idempotency_operations.operation_scope<br/>plus api_idempotency_operations.idempotency_key (not a row lock)
    Note over A,D: Same operation_scope and idempotency_key.<br/>Applies to prices, credits, limits, purchases,<br/>cancellations and invoice catch-up.
    A->>D: BEGIN, scoped advisory request lock
    A->>D: SELECT api_idempotency_operations, no result yet
    B->>D: BEGIN, same scoped advisory request lock
    Note over B,D: WAIT for A's transaction to end
    alt A succeeds
        A->>D: Acquire the operation's business lock<br/>Apply changes, store request and response
        A->>D: COMMIT effect and api_idempotency_operations
        D-->>B: Scoped lock granted
        B->>D: Read original stored response
        alt Same semantic input
            B->>D: COMMIT without taking business locks
            Note over B,D: Return original result, no repeated effect
        else Changed semantic input
            B->>D: ROLLBACK, return 409 billing_conflict
        end
    else A fails before commit
        A->>D: ROLLBACK effect and request-history insertion
        D-->>B: Scoped lock granted, history still absent
        B->>D: Validate and execute its own command normally
    end
    Note over A,D: Different scopes or keys usually have separate request locks.<br/>Hash collisions can cause waiting, but identity uses the full key.<br/>Same-customer commands can still meet at the account lock.
